WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.080
Please welcome Alicia Vikander!

00:00:18.040 --> 00:00:20.160
-Welcome to the show.
-Thank you.

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:27.320
We saw some scenes from a film
that will be in theaters soon,

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:31.800
both here in Sweden and in Norway.

00:00:31.960 --> 00:00:38.000
This is one of several
international films you're starring in.

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:42.080
-But this one is in Danish.
-That's right, it's in Danish.

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:45.360
I had to learn a new language.

00:00:45.520 --> 00:00:48.440
What was that like?
Was it a challenge?

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:54.080
God, yes. I recall getting the audition
scene from a Swedish casting director.

00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:59.920
I said, "There must have been a mistake.
You know I don't speak Danish."

00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:04.720
She said, "Do what you want.
Do it in Swedish or in Danish."

00:01:04.880 --> 00:01:10.880
A friend's mother who's half-Danish
recorded the whole scene on my iPhone.

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:14.840
Then I tried my best
to learn it phonetically.

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:19.160
Then I got my last callback
in Copenhagen.

00:01:19.320 --> 00:01:24.320
-I got to meet Mads and the director.
-Mads Mikkelsen, your co-star?

00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:28.440
We did a screen test,
which is when you're in full costume

00:01:28.600 --> 00:01:32.640
and we shot some scenes on a set.

00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:35.120
Afterwards I went back to my hotel.

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:40.240
I was so tired I didn't even take calls
from my mother or my agent.

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:47.400
I wanted this part so badly
I had almost developed tunnel vision.

00:01:47.560 --> 00:01:50.080
But I thought, no way I'll get it.

00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:53.000
But then the director
called me and asked

00:01:53.160 --> 00:01:58.160
if he could come by the hotel
for a beer, and I said, sure.

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:02.920
So there we were, and I was so tired,
having expended all this energy

00:02:03.080 --> 00:02:06.240
trying to understand Danish
this whole day.

00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:10.560
And he started talking,
and it was full of people, it was loud,

00:02:10.720 --> 00:02:14.800
and he said something and I thought,
I can't say "what?" again

00:02:14.960 --> 00:02:19.360
so I'll just have to do
the classic nod and smile.

00:02:19.520 --> 00:02:23.920
-As we all do when people speak Danish.
-Like I've done with you.

00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:30.400
No, but he said something
and saw it didn't quite land.

00:02:30.560 --> 00:02:34.080
So he said it again,
and I laughed a little.

00:02:34.240 --> 00:02:39.520
And he went, "OK, I'll take it
in English. I just gave you the part."

00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:46.680
I was so happy he hadn't just run out
thinking, "this will never work."

00:02:46.840 --> 00:02:52.800
I was so happy, and I got up
to my room and put the TV on,

00:02:52.960 --> 00:02:56.320
and I totally panicked
because I didn't get a word.

00:02:56.480 --> 00:03:01.000
And I thought, I've got 2.5 months
to learn Danish like I promised.

00:03:01.160 --> 00:03:07.200
It worked out. The film&lt;i&gt;A Royal Affair&lt;/i&gt;
premiered at the Berlin Film Festival.

00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:10.840
-It won two awards.
-Yes. We were so happy.

00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:13.640
-You played the queen?
-Yes.

00:03:13.800 --> 00:03:20.000
Don't worry, even Danes are having
a harder time understanding Danes.

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:24.640
The language is changing,
becoming more unintelligible.

00:03:24.800 --> 00:03:28.120
It can affect at what age
they learn to speak.

00:03:28.280 --> 00:03:32.960
It's scientifically proven. Almost.

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:38.000
For us Norwegians, to sit here
and trash-talk the Danes, it's...

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:41.680
Good thing they're so nice,
given how they talk.

00:03:41.840 --> 00:03:46.600
-We think they're nice. We don't know.
-I'm pretty sure.

00:03:46.760 --> 00:03:49.520
-You started out as a ballet dancer.
-Yes.

00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:54.760
-You studied ballet for nine years.
-Yes, that's right.

00:03:54.920 --> 00:04:01.720
I attended The Royal Swedish
Ballet School from the age of ten.

00:04:01.880 --> 00:04:03.880
Why did you give it up?

00:04:04.040 --> 00:04:08.800
It was an incredibly hard decision,
after so many years.

00:04:08.960 --> 00:04:15.000
I was totally focused on it,
dancing six days a week for hours.

00:04:15.160 --> 00:04:18.280
That was my goal for many years.

00:04:18.440 --> 00:04:23.960
But in the last few years,
it began to dawn on me

00:04:24.120 --> 00:04:28.640
that my passion lay elsewhere.

00:04:30.080 --> 00:04:33.800
But it's still... I mean,
I never went to acting school.

00:04:33.960 --> 00:04:37.400
I applied numerous times
but never made the cut.

00:04:37.560 --> 00:04:44.360
But all those years are still
a foundation for my current profession.

00:04:44.520 --> 00:04:49.480
It's something I revisit
and still lean on.

00:04:49.640 --> 00:04:52.600
You're making
several international films.

00:04:52.760 --> 00:04:56.200
-And you travel a lot.
-Yes.

00:04:56.360 --> 00:05:00.240
It seems to me,
when we've tried to get hold of you,

00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:04.040
you're either in Hollywood
or in Copenhagen,

00:05:04.200 --> 00:05:07.160
or in London...
You're constantly traveling.

00:05:07.320 --> 00:05:09.960
Yes, I've been on the road 13-14 months.

00:05:10.120 --> 00:05:13.480
You've become good
at packing a suitcase, I bet.

00:05:13.640 --> 00:05:19.680
Just two weeks ago I was in Siberia,
Russia, shooting in -38 degrees.

00:05:19.840 --> 00:05:25.080
Siberia? Not very many Hollywood stars
tend to go there.

00:05:25.240 --> 00:05:32.040
Well... We recently wrapped shooting
for&lt;i&gt;Anna Karenina,&lt;/i&gt;

00:05:32.200 --> 00:05:35.120
directed by Joe Wright
and due out this fall.

00:05:35.280 --> 00:05:40.040
-Keira Knightley stars as Anna Karenina?
-Yes. I spoke to her yesterday.

00:05:40.200 --> 00:05:43.120
She was very happy
about not going to Russia.

00:05:43.280 --> 00:05:46.880
-She didn't go. But you did?
-Yes, me and this other...

00:05:47.040 --> 00:05:48.800
I felt fortunate.

00:05:48.960 --> 00:05:54.000
I went with Domnhall Gleeson,
who plays Levin, my husband.

00:05:54.160 --> 00:05:57.160
We were supposed to stay
at a nice hotel,

00:05:57.320 --> 00:06:02.160
but instead we stayed in a house
that was only used in the summertime.

00:06:02.320 --> 00:06:07.760
They'd never tried to heat it up
in the winter. It was freezing cold.

00:06:07.920 --> 00:06:12.160
The last night I slept on the floor
in the bigger house,

00:06:12.320 --> 00:06:17.680
next to the fireplace,
because I was dying from the cold.

00:06:17.840 --> 00:06:21.080
There was this one guy from the crew...

00:06:21.240 --> 00:06:26.400
He had to go to the outhouse,
which was the toilet facility they had.

00:06:26.560 --> 00:06:31.000
He said, "I'll be quick, one pair
of pants and a sweater will do."

00:06:31.160 --> 00:06:34.960
And he went, did his business,
and when he opened the door

00:06:35.120 --> 00:06:39.760
he realizes there are
twelve wolves out there.

00:06:39.920 --> 00:06:45.840
So he starts to yell,
but no one in the house can hear him.

00:06:46.000 --> 00:06:50.120
But after ten minutes, they go,
"He's taking a long time."

00:06:50.280 --> 00:06:55.640
So they see the wolves, grab guns,
and when they find him he had frostbite.

00:06:55.800 --> 00:07:00.720
So they had to call an ambulance.

00:07:00.880 --> 00:07:05.000
This is not what I imagined
Hollywood life to be like.

00:07:05.160 --> 00:07:06.800
Right! Totally normal.

00:07:06.960 --> 00:07:13.040
You're off to shoot a new film now?

00:07:13.200 --> 00:07:18.280
Yes, we're in pre-production
for "Seventh Son" now.

00:07:18.440 --> 00:07:21.320
And that's some sort of action...?

00:07:21.480 --> 00:07:25.840
It's a big fantasy,
gothic, action-like film.

00:07:26.000 --> 00:07:30.160
-Who else is in it?
-Jeff Bridges and Julianne Moore.

00:07:30.320 --> 00:07:34.040
-And Ben Barnes.
-Don't those names feel surreal to you?

00:07:34.200 --> 00:07:38.120
Like, seriously?! Really?!
Do you ever think like that?

00:07:38.280 --> 00:07:41.840
Sure, it's a bit of a shock.

00:07:42.000 --> 00:07:45.480
The first shock is probably
the biggest shock.

00:07:45.640 --> 00:07:51.320
Once you have lunch together or
go to Siberia with people, it's like...

00:07:51.480 --> 00:07:54.680
Totally normal?

00:07:54.840 --> 00:07:57.720
The wolves still aren't very normal.

